  "verdict_reason": "The design is implementable and the options it lists are not wrong, but it leaves R11's own required change (activity judged by the recorded pid + start time only) untouched, which turns the panel into a source of false refusals and real numbering collisions, so the framing should be re-decided before acceptance.",
  "reply_markdown": "**Verified (HEAD 3bd8a83, read-only).** Panel loop and member spec (`codex-consult.ps1:835-1010`), single-run critical section: lock, pending, numbering (1495-1620), commit order (2451 ingest -> 2585 reply -> 2590 findings -> 2669 sessions), `Get-NextNumbers` (common:750), `Add-ReplyFindings` (1284), `Test-PendingActive` (4721), `Find-CodexProcesses` (4624), `Get-CollabSnapshot`/`Compare-DirectorySnapshot` (624/654), `Get-EndpointHealth` (3661), `Select-ParentThread` (4208), `codex-findings.ps1` (360-433), fakes (`tests/fake-codex.ps1:26`). **Inferred, not executed:** wall clock, provider quota correlation, concurrent agy sign-in, `Start-Process` redirect encoding.\n\n**Framing.** The brief answers *how do N members share one task's mutable state* and never asks whether they must. Design points 2, 4, 5, 6, 7 exist only because members write the two stores and `handoffs/` themselves. Options it does not list: (a) per-member staging files plus one ordered commit by the parent, which is already the sole lock holder - this deletes the new write lock, the lost-update class and the Q2 residual; (b) name that staging directory `.consult.panel-<id>/`, because `Get-CollabSnapshot` already skips `.consult.*` (common:639), so an agy member's collab check needs *no* exemption at all - stricter than ROADMAP R11:193 asks; (c) members as sibling task directories under `.consult.panel-<id>/`, each running today's code untouched; (d) an append-only store (one file per consult) that needs no lock. To choose I would need to know: do provider quotas bind per account window (then all-at-once loses whole panels); does R12 `-Detach` land before this design (it wants the reserving process to return at once - incompatible with lock-holder = waiter/committer unless the holder is a nanny); and is R11's acceptance target only *wall clock = max*, or *max with no new failure modes*.\n\n**Q1** Sound for additive deltas (ids are deterministic from NN, common:1319; checks append; ledger insert by n), but not free: the design's own kill guard and 60 s give-up widen the window between findings.json and sessions.json, and the handoff is written *before* the commit from the stale ingest (2451 -> 2585), so its rendered section can contradict what the fresh store commits (supersedes resolution, retained blockers, prior statuses). Staging plus one ordered parent commit is safer: single writer, no write lock, and the parent already prints the summary. Its cost - a process that must outlive the panel - is one R12 will impose anyway.\n\n**Q2** The residual is self-inflicted. With staging, nothing a member writes is inside the monitored tree, so the stores stay monitored for free and no sibling prefixes are exempted. If members stay writers, at least keep the exemption to the ROADMAP minimum: the siblings' handoff files only.\n\n**Q3** No. Activity is judged by the *reviewer* process: the recorded bridge pid is used only as a parent for the descendant scan (common:4790), and once the reviewer exits nothing codex-like is alive, so a member still committing reads INACTIVE - a new run may then start (parent dead means no lock), find no ledger entry, handoff or finding for that NN, re-assign the same NN and write the same reply paths (finding #1). R11:187-188 already demands the survivor scan use the recorded pid + start time only; the design never touches `Test-PendingActive`. Do that, add the member's own bridge pid + start to its record, and an orphan member becomes safe to let finish.\n\n**Q4** Neither all-at-once nor 1: the knob is a count, but the constraint is a key. Default to one member per endpoint fingerprint, endpoints in parallel - two members of one plan fail together (finding #4).\n\n**Q5** One real assumption: `Select-ParentThread` walks the array backwards and takes the first lineage match (common:4245), so newest thread = last entry; with n-ordered insertion that becomes the highest-n member, which in a wave may have finished first (finding #9). `Get-NextNumbers:753` uses `@($Consults).Count` only as a floor; `Read-AllTaskConsults` flattens; `Get-EndpointHealth` sorts by `when` (order-independent, but `when` is the run start - finding #3); the scoreboard sorts by name/lineage/purpose; `codex-findings.ps1` builds `LedgerNs` from the entries, so ORPHAN detection is order-independent.\n\n## Requested checks\nRC1 (scratch clone, workspace-write, ~15 min): in a pane run the design's own case parent-killed-mid-panel, letting member 1's reviewer exit but delaying its commit, then kill the parent; in a second pane run `-Panel -DryRun` and read the n/NN it prints and the n the next real run takes. If a live orphan member's NN is re-assigned, finding #1 is confirmed.\nRC2 (scratch clone, workspace-write, ~5 min): leave a member record in state `running` with a dead child while a second fake codex sleeps 30 s, then run `codex-findings.ps1 -Task <t> -Id F01-1 -Status implemented`. A refusal naming that sleeping process confirms finding #2.\nRC3 (read-only, 2 min, any shell with the scripts): dot-source `codex-consult-common.ps1` and call `Get-EndpointHealth -Consults @(<entry A: fingerprint F, when t0, quota failure>, <entry B: fingerprint F, when t0+1s, usable reply>) -Fingerprint F < -UtcNow t0+5m >`. An empty `Quota` confirms finding #3.\nRC4 (scratch clone, workspace-write, ~5 min): run one panel member with stdout redirected to a file as the design proposes, then compare the file's bytes with the same member run through a pipe. Any change in the captured text settles the unproven encoding item.",
